# Results manifest: what each file is and how to read a method name

All numbers are computed by `scripts/02_run.py` (experiments, raw predictions in `results/raw/*.pkl`, not committed) and `scripts/03_analyze.py` (tables below). Scores are fractions in [0,1]; multiply by 100 for points. Every row says its benchmark, split and n (items); N is in `per_bench.csv.gz`.

## Method names: `<design>|<estimator group>`
Design (how the n items are chosen, from source models only): `uniform` (SRS), `strat` (items sorted by source difficulty, one random item per stratum of N/n), `pps` (inclusion prob. ∝ √(p̂(1−p̂)) mixed with ε uniform, systematic PPS in difficulty order; ε chosen on sources), `balanced` (greedy forward selection from a random pool of 4n items so the subset mean matches the sources' full scores), `anchor` (k-means with n clusters on item response vectors, medoid per cluster, weight=cluster size), `infotop` (top-n items by 2PL information; deterministic), `cat` (adaptive max-information item selection per target with the d=1 2PL; no draw randomness).
Estimator group (from the n answers of the target): `mean` (Hájek weighted mean), `calreg` (OLS of sources' full score on their subset score), `irt-model` / `knn-model` / `ridge-model` (mean over all N items of the predicted outcomes; the predictor is fitted on sources and the target's n answers: `irt` = logistic latent-factor model, d=2, MAP ability; `knn` = k nearest sources by agreement on the sample, `ridge` = linear kernel ridge from sample responses to the full response vector), `*-plugin` (observed answers on the sample + model prediction on the unsampled items), `diff-irt|knn|ridge` (difference estimator: model mean over all items + λ × weighted mean of the 5-fold cross-fitted residuals on the sample), `diffnaive-irt` (same with non-cross-fitted residuals at λ=1; diagnostic), `fixed:<name>` (hyper-parameters fixed to λ=1; diagnostic of the cross-fitting bias).
Hyper-parameters (ε, k, ridge α, λ) are picked per (benchmark, split fold, n, design, group) on an inner split of the *sources* (see PROTOCOL.md); the pickled records keep the choice (`chosen`) and its inner RMSE.

## Files (results/)
- `audit.csv` — per benchmark: N, models kept after dedup, dropped duplicates, score range, families (Table 1).
- `targets.csv` — per (benchmark, split): k targets, number of independent families, thin flag (<10 targets or <5 families).
- `per_bench.csv.gz` — per (bench, split, n, method): `rmse` (sqrt of mean over targets and draws of squared error), `mae`, `bias` (mean signed error), `bias2` (mean over targets of squared draw-mean error), `var` (mean over targets of draw variance, ddof=1; 0 for the deterministic designs), `ratio` = rmse / rmse of `uniform|mean`, `thin`.
- `primary.csv` (n=100) and `ratio_by_n.csv.gz` — geometric mean over benchmarks of the RMSE ratio per (split, n, method), against `baseline` = `uniform|mean` (the primary endpoint) or `strat|mean` (`ratio_vs_strat_mean` in `per_bench`), with 95 % family-bootstrap interval (2000 replicates; families resampled within each benchmark; flavour `non_thin` excludes thin cells, `all` includes them); `n_bench` benchmarks entering. Benchmarks are not independent (HELM models recur across its benchmarks; OLL families recur across OLL benchmarks): the interval reflects target-family sampling only.
- `ordering.csv.gz` — Spearman, Kendall τ-b, top-decile precision and top-decile Kendall per (bench, split, n, method); computed per (fold, draw) over that fold's targets, then averaged.
- `pairwise.csv.gz` — pairwise order accuracy by bin of the true score gap (|gap| bins in score points: 0–0.5, 0.5–1, 1–2, 2–4, 4–8, >8 as `gap_bin` 0..5) and `ref_acc`, the sample-only reference: probability that n uniformly sampled items order a pair correctly given only that pair's own answers, Φ(|gap|/sd) with sd² = (disagreement − gap²)(1−n/N)/n computed from the pair's full vectors. It is a reference for plain SRS, not a bound for source-aided methods.
- `bias_by_score.csv.gz` — signed error and RMSE by target position (z-score of the target's true score against its fold's source scores).
- `ceiling.csv` — per (bench, split, n), uniform design: Brier score on the unsampled items of the constant predictor (`brier_const` = μ(1−μ) with the target's true μ), of the source item means (`brier_p`), of `irt` and of 10-NN predictions; `q_* = 1 − brier_*/brier_const` (fraction of Bernoulli variance explained).
- `pilot_d.csv` — source-only inner-validation pilot choosing the `irt` latent dimension (d=2).
- `results/raw/target_level.parquet` (all n; a release asset, not in git; ~10^6 rows) — one row per (bench, split, n, method, target model): `true_score`, `est_mean` (estimate averaged over the subset draws), `bias`, `draw_var`, `sq_err` (mean over draws of squared error), `n_draws`, `family`, `fold`, `chosen` (hyper-parameter variant picked on sources), `n_over_N`, `qualifies_extrap` (every extrap target is strictly above every source of its fold by construction). Per-draw predictions are in `results/raw/*.pkl` (release asset): a row per draw would be ~10^7 rows.

Splits: `interp`, `lineage`, `extrap` (targets = top 25 % of models), and the sensitivity splits `extrap10`, `extrap50` (top 10 % / 50 %). Files are CSV or CSV.gz (gzip) as named.

## Added after the main runs
- `results/bias_bands.csv` (n = 100; from `scripts/05_bands.py`): mean signed error per (split, method, `pos_bin`) pooled over non-thin benchmarks with a 95 % family-bootstrap interval (`ci_lo`, `ci_hi`; 2000 replicates, families resampled inside each benchmark); `k` targets, `n_bench` benchmarks contributing to the bin (position = (true score − mean source score)/sd of source scores).
- `results/pairwise_bands.csv` (n = 100): pairwise order accuracy per (split, method, `gap_bin`), pooled over non-thin benchmarks (weights = pairs), with `ref_acc` the pooled analytic sample-only reference; interval = bootstrap over benchmarks (≤ 13 units, so coarse; pairs mix families, so no family bootstrap).
- `results/msrc.csv` (`scripts/04_msrc.py`): RMSE ratio to `uniform|mean` at n = 100 versus number of source models M (`M` = 0 means all), designs uniform/strat/pps/balanced, interp and extrap; same bootstrap as `primary.csv`. Run when `results/raw_msrc` is complete.
- `results/twopct/` (`TWO_PCT=1 python3 scripts/03_analyze.py results/raw_2pct results/twopct`): the same files for the secondary endpoint n/N = 2 % (n = round(0.02 N) per benchmark; label `n` = 0, actual n in `n_actual` of `per_bench.csv.gz`). Run when `results/raw_2pct` is complete.

## Estimator names added later (`--extra` runs; `results/raw_extra`, `results/extra_*`)
- `irtw-model`, `irtw-plugin`, `irtw-diff`: the `irt` predictor with a weak prior on the ability (precision 0.1 instead of 1) — the check that the MAP shrinkage is not what makes the model-based predictor biased in extrapolation.
- `recal-{s|q}[irt][0]` (target-recalibrated regression estimator; weights 1/π): `y_i ~ β0 + β1 x_i (+ β2 p̂_i)` on the target's n answers, estimate = mean of the fitted values over all N items. `s` uses `x = logit(source mean accuracy of item i)`; `q` uses `x = c + A·mean ability` of the source-fitted IRT (the IRT logit of the item for an average source); `irt` adds the cross-fitted IRT prediction as second feature; the suffix `0` means no ridge (κ = 0), otherwise κ = 1. The regression falls back to the Hájek mean if either outcome class has < 3 sampled items or the weighted design is rank-deficient; the pickles hold the per-target flag (`diag["fb:{design}:{name}"]`) and `08_recal.py` writes the rate by (split, bench, n, design, estimator).

## Files added later
- `selected.csv`, `twopct/selected.csv` (`06_select.py`): leave-one-benchmark-out selection of one method per class (anchor; design-based = `mean`/`calreg`; difference-corrected; info/adaptive selection; model-based; with `--extra` also target-recalibrated). Columns `split, cls, held_out, suite, baseline, chosen, ratio, ci_lo, ci_hi, n_bench`. `held_out` = a benchmark name: the method chosen on the other non-thin benchmarks and its ratio on that benchmark; `ALL`: the pooled geometric mean of those held-out ratios with a 95 % family bootstrap (the "selected without the held-out benchmark" number; `chosen` = "k distinct, mode …"); `POSTHOC`: the method chosen on all benchmarks (optimistic). `suite` is `all`, `OLL` or `HELM`. `baseline` is `uniform|mean` or `strat|mean`.
- `primary_suite.csv`, `twopct/primary_suite.csv`: `primary.csv` by suite (`all`, `OLL`, `HELM`).
- `per_bench.csv.gz` also has `n_draws` and `bias2_unbiased = bias2 − var/n_draws` (the draw-mean squared bias with its Monte-Carlo variance removed; can be slightly negative).
- `inner50/`: the same files as the main results for the larger-gap inner split (extrap, n = 100, four designs).
- `inner50/paired_vs_main.csv` (`scripts/09_paired.py`): per method, the RMSE ratio to `uniform|mean` under the main inner split (`ratio_a`) and the larger-gap split (`ratio_b`), and `b_over_a` = geometric mean over the 13 non-thin benchmarks of ratio_b/ratio_a with a 95% family bootstrap (`ci_lo`, `ci_hi`) in which both runs share the family resample; < 1 means the larger-gap split is better. Methods whose fit does not use the inner split give exactly 1.
- `ceiling_centred.csv` (`scripts/10_ceiling_centred.py`): per (bench, split) at n = 100 on the uniform design: `brier_const`, `brier_irt` (as in `ceiling.csv`), `centred` = Brier of the IRT predictor minus the squared mean residual on the unsampled items (computed per target and draw), `q`, `q_c` = 1 − Brier/`brier_const` and 1 − `centred`/`brier_const`, `ref` = √(1−q), `ref_c` = √(1−q_c), `obs` = observed RMSE ratio of `uniform|diff-irt` to `uniform|mean`, `thin`.
- `extrap_bias.csv` (`scripts/11_extrap_bias.py`): extrapolation split, n = 100, non-thin benchmarks; per design and model-based estimator: `slope`, `intercept` of the draw-mean signed bias on the target's gap above the lowest target of its benchmark (both in units of the benchmark's sd of target scores), `bias_top` (mean of the top quarter by gap), `share_bias2` (squared bias over squared bias plus draw variance), `n_targets`.
- `public_baselines.csv` (`scripts/13_public_compare.py`): per (bench, split, stage, method): `rmse` pooled over the targets of the folds run (`folds`, `n_targets`), `ratio_uniform_mean` to `uniform|mean` on the same targets, `source` (`public` = the public code's recipe, `ours` = our re-implementation or reference from `results/raw`), `draws` (selection seeds of a public method).
- `highR/`: R = 100 run (see REPRODUCE.md). `highR_vs_main.csv` (`scripts/14_bias_r100.py`): per (split, method) at n = 100 over the non-thin benchmarks, the mean of `b2` (`bias2_unbiased`), `v` (variance) and `mse`, each over the MSE of `uniform|mean` of the same run and benchmark (Table 3's quantities), for R = 10 (`*_R10`) and R = 100 (`*_R100`), with the standard error over benchmarks (`*_se_*`) and the difference (`*_diff`). `highR_target_bias.csv`: per (split, method), pooled over targets: `median_abs_bias`, `median_mc_se` (= sqrt(draw_var / n_draws)), `share_abs_bias_gt_2se`, `share_bias2_of_mse` (sum of bias² − MC variance over sum of squared error).
- `kmstrong.csv`: per (bench, split, method) RMSE ratio to `uniform|mean` with the light (`ratio_light`) and strong (`ratio_strong`) anchor k-means; the two columns use different draws.
- `recal_fallback_*.csv`: fall-back rates of the recalibration regression.
- Output directories of the extra runs: `extra_n100/`, `extra_2pct/` (the main schema, estimators `recal-*`, `irtw-*` and the same designs).
- `twopct/` also has `n_actual` in `per_bench.csv.gz`.
